@reject_offer
title: It seems we can't come to an agreement. 
new-page: true
on-arrival: sdi_relation -= 5; left_m_relation -= 5; centre_m_relation -= 5; right_m_relation -= 10; lsr_relation -= 5; centre_sr_relation -= 10; right_sr_relation -= 10; ns_relation -= 10; democraticity -= 1; instability += 4; moderate_dissent += 10; leninist_dissent -= 5; leninist_strength += 5; leftcom_dissent -= 5; vikzhel_unrest += 20; right_sr_strength += 2; right_m_strength += 2; 

= Negotiations Collapse

Unable to come to an agreement, we're forced to walk away from the negotiations. The other parties express their strong condemnation, as do a number of other interest groups and organizations. The Bolshevik Moderates lament the failure, though their passion for cooperation is no less deterred. 

Lenin criticizes the entire endeavor, using the failure of talks to prove his point that compromise is impossible. This will likely serve as a rallying cry for the Leninist position going forward. 

Vikzhel immediately unleashes a full-scale strike across the railway network, and many other prominent unions and enterprises decide to join them. 




@accept_offer
title: Agree to the offer. 
unavailable-subtitle: The other parties will not accept the current offer. 
choose-if: influence > 43
new-page: true
on-arrival: lenin_anger = 1 if (commissariats_held < 10 or disarm_workers = 1 or bar_lenin = 1 or restructure = 1 or drop_chair = 1 or drop_foreign = 1 or drop_military = 1 or drop_internal = 1);

= Tentative Offer

After days of tough negotiations, there is a viable offer on the table. Some Bolshevik newspapers even preemptively acclaim the formation of a new coalition government. 

[?if lenin_anger = 1: Lenin however is absolutely furious that we have even entertained such a prospect, especially upon reading the proposed terms. In a heated party meeting, he berates the Party negotiators and outright accuses them of treason. So strongly does he feel about this, he declares he'd sooner let the Party split over the issue, or "go to the sailors" and crush the concilators by force. ?]

[?if lenin_anger != 1: Lenin however is absolutely furious that we have even entertained such a prospect, though the measured agreement is at least some consolation. ?]

The Leninists push for breaking off talks entirely, though its possible we could cautiously reopen negotiations. If so, it's now clear that anything less than this would be a non-starter: 
<ul>
Recognition of the inviolability of the decrees/actions of the 2nd Soviet Congress.
Recognition of the CEC as the coutnry's sole supreme authority.
CEC membership only to groups previously represented in the soviets (i.e. no expanded "People's Council").
Lenin and Trotsky must remain in government.<br>
The Bolsheviks must retain a preponderance of government posts (~75%).
</ul>

- @reopen_negotiations
- @break_talks


@break_talks
title: Break-off negotiations.
new-page: true
on-arrival: sdi_relation -= 10; left_m_relation -= 10; centre_m_relation -= 10; right_m_relation -= 15; lsr_relation -= 10; centre_sr_relation -= 15; right_sr_relation -= 15; ns_relation -= 15; democraticity -= 2; instability += 6; vikzhel_unrest += 20; moderate_dissent += 10; leninist_dissent -= 5; leftcom_dissent -= 5; right_sr_strength += 5; right_m_strength += 5; 

Our sudden renegation on the agreement and breaking-off of negotiations entirely shocks the other parties, seemingly proving the right socialists correct after all—more than likely compromise will be impossible from here on out. Likewise, we receive immense condemnation from various interest groups, and even some members of our own party.

Though on the other hand, Lenin's argument against cooperation carries some sway in the working-class districts of the capital, with workers' councils agreeing that they cannot compromise the values and gains of the October Revolution. 

Vikzhel immediately unleashes a full-scale strike across the railway network, and many other prominent unions and enterprises decide to join them. 




@reopen_negotiations
title: Reopen negotiations, presenting the Party's minimum requirements to the others as an "ultimatum". 
go-to: menu2
new-page: true
on-arrival: commissariats_held = 14; influence = (10 + (left_m_relation / 3) + (moderate_strength / 10) - (instability / 10)); truce_offer = 0; CEC_membership = 0; CEC_rebalance = 0; socialist_coalition = 0; subordinate = 0; restructure = 0; drop_chair = 0; drop_foreign = 0; drop_military = 0; drop_enlightenment = 0; drop_internal = 0; drop_railways = 0; drop_trade = 0; drop_finance = 0; drop_labor = 0; drop_justice = 0; drop_agriculture = 0; drop_foodsupply = 0; drop_posts = 0; drop_nationalities = 0; drop_welfare = 0;

Reneging on our earlier verbal agreement aggravates the other parties, strengthening the obstinence of many right socialists. Though the more time stalls, news of anti-Bolshevik military defeats weakens their negotiating power. The Left-SRs meanwhile issue a formal statement criticizing inflexibility within the Bolshevik camp. 


